+      "jumpstartPrompt": "You are helping O'keito S. shape the Staff-Level AI Assessment for MarvelUs AU. The first market is Melbourne small and medium businesses, starting with trades, construction, and maintenance firms where quoting, proposals, follow-ups, and admin consume staff time.\n\n## One-liner\nWe ask your staff about their real working day, in minutes, then show you in dollars where time is leaking and which fixes pay for themselves.\n\n## Source\nTKO Ep. #344, 3 College Kids Help Companies Use AI for $15K/Month.\nhttps://anchor.fm/s/10796c924/podcast/play/127567226/https%3A%2F%2Fd3ctxlq1ktw2nl.cloudfront.net%2Fstaging%2F2026-9-16%2F434295710-44100-2-ad14bd7e896f1.mp3\n\nTreat the episode proof points as source claims, not independently verified results.\n\n## How it works\n1. Owner call: understand the business, bottlenecks, and which staff roles should be included.\n2. Staff survey: send an anonymous 10-question survey that ends with booking a follow-up call.\n3. Staff interviews: run recorded 15-minute interviews with an AI note taker. Frame the work as tasks, not jobs, and keep names out of the report.\n4. Priced report: group tasks by role, calculate annual hours and costs, then rank the opportunities by value and likely return.\n5. Fixes: use redacted real paperwork to demonstrate practical improvements, then scope the MarvelUs fixes, including digital support, SEO, and automations.\n\n## Method\nCapture each repeated task as times per week × minutes each.\nWeekly hours = times per week × minutes each ÷ 60.\nAnnual cost = weekly hours × 52 × hourly pay rate.\nUse the business's own loaded hourly pay rate where available. Recovered time is capacity until the business puts it to productive use.\n\n## Top 5 staff questions\n1. What job do you do most often in a week? Record times per week and minutes each.\n2. What information do you type in more than once? Record the systems, times per week, and minutes each.\n3. What do you wait on before you can finish work? Record times per week, minutes lost, and the usual wait length.\n4. Tell me about the last mistake or redo. What happened, and how long did the fix take?\n5. Dream fix: if you could make one task disappear or run itself, what would it be?\n\n## Three lengths\n1. Full discovery: 26 staff questions covering the week, repeated typing, searching, waiting, chasing, mistakes, rework, customers, quoting, and desired fixes.\n2. Guided interview: a 15-minute conversation using the highest-value questions and a final read-back of the top three tasks and numbers.\n3. Pre-interview survey: 10 questions completed before the call, ending with a booking link for the 15-minute follow-up.\n\n## Proof from the episode\n- The construction client reportedly did about $30 million a year.\n- It bought a paid assessment for about $1,000.\n- Staff completed a survey followed by recorded calls with an AI note taker.\n- A maintenance proposal reportedly fell from about one hour to about five minutes.\n- Retainer options were US$10,000, US$15,000, and US$25,000 per month, and the client selected US$15,000 per month.\n- Two similar-sized prospects were reportedly in the pipeline, plus an invitation to assess a $12 billion company.\n\n## Pricing\nPricing: TBD. O sets the assessment and post-assessment pricing before the offer is approved. Do not publish suggested prices or imply that pricing has been approved.\n\n## Existing files\nThe working files are saved in Obsidian at `Grok-Bot/Biz/staff-questionnaire-service/`.\n\n## Next steps\n1. O sets pricing.\n2. Reel builds the sales deck from the brief and question sets.\n3. Approve the offer and outreach before anything is sent.\n\n## Deliverables\n1. Finalise the assessment scope and pricing after O's decision.\n2. Build a concise sales deck covering the problem, workflow, method, top questions, three lengths, proof, deliverables, and pricing.\n3. Create an anonymous report template with task, role, frequency, minutes, annual hours, annual cost, opportunity rank, and expected return.\n4. Draft the offer and outreach for approval, aimed first at suitable Melbourne trades, construction, and maintenance businesses.\n5. Define a first-client pilot with success measures for staff participation, hours identified, opportunities ranked, demos produced, and fixes approved.\n\nKeep staff trust central. Explain that the assessment studies tasks rather than jobs, removes names from reporting, and needs real examples and counted events instead of vague estimates. Write all customer-facing copy without em dashes or en dashes."
