+      "jumpstartPrompt": "You are helping O'keito S. turn Clay Lawrence's five local marketing offers into a focused MarvelUs offer stack for Melbourne businesses.\n\n## Source\nYouTube: 5 Boring But High Paying Local Marketing Offers That Never Fail! by Clay Lawrence, published 11 Oct 2026.\nhttps://youtu.be/tbhoenh1lkE\n\nTreat performance claims from the video as unverified. Keep all source prices in USD and all MarvelUs recommendations in AUD.\n\n## Five source offers and prices in USD\n1. Google Local Service Ads management: $300 to $1,000 per month. Clay recommends $300 to $500 per month. Manage service areas and dispute bad leads. Results weaken when reviews are poor or the business does not answer calls and messages.\n2. Google Business Profile management: $300 to $500 per month. The recurring work covers photos, posts, services, and corrections, while much of the ranking gain comes from the initial optimisation.\n3. Reviews and reputation management: $100 to $500 per month. A widget-only option is $99 per month. Automate review requests and responses, repurpose reviews, and place the review widget on the website.\n4. Websites: $3,000 to $5,000 upfront, or spread the cost over two years, plus $100 per month hosting after the build is paid. Include revisions and analytics.\n5. Old lead and customer re-contact by SMS and email: $1,000 to $2,000 setup, a tracked revenue-share option, or $1,500 to $2,000 per quarter for home services. Avoid exhausting small lists by contacting them too often.\n\n## Suggested MarvelUs offer stack in AUD\n### 1. Reviews entry offer\nPrice at A$199 to A$399 per month, with a widget-only tier at A$129 per month. Digital support asks for a review after each completed job and runs the follow-up workflow.\n\n### 2. Old-list re-contact\nDigital support contacts old leads and customers by SMS and email and books replies. Price at A$1,500 to A$2,500 per quarter. Pilot the first suitable client on revenue share, track attributable revenue, then agree a fair split. Confirm consent and comply with the Spam Act 2003 before sending.\n\n### 3. Website plus Google profile rebuild\nTarget Melbourne fabrication and engineering shops in Laverton, Derrimut, and Dandenong. Price the website at A$3,500 to A$6,000, or about A$199 per month over 24 months, then A$119 per month hosting. Offer one-time Google Business Profile setup at A$750.\n\n## Six useful script lines, summarised\n1. Website upsell: explain that new traffic is reaching the current website but dying there, so the site must give visitors a stronger path to enquire.\n2. Website value: promise a polished conversion-focused site structured so Google can understand what the business should rank for.\n3. Profile expectations: tell service-area businesses plainly that they are harder to rank.\n4. Revenue-share risk reversal: offer to run re-contact without an upfront service fee, track produced revenue, and then agree the split.\n5. Discovery: ask for the biggest marketing headache, where customers currently come from, and what should be automated but is not.\n6. Local opener: say you are researching how to help nearby businesses and offer to buy the owner coffee or lunch.\n\nUse diagnose-then-prescribe logic. If payroll cash is urgent, lead with old-list re-contact. If there is budget and a need for fast growth, consider Local Service Ads. If the goal is sustained owned growth, combine the Google profile, reviews, and website.\n\n## Immediate checklist\n- [ ] Add the reviews offer to marvelus.cc.\n- [ ] Test the offer with this week's 8 Australian prospects.\n- [ ] Pick one revenue-share pilot client with a usable old contact list.\n\n## Deliverables\n1. Write the reviews offer page for marvelus.cc with scope, exclusions, pricing tiers, onboarding requirements, and a direct call to action.\n2. Create the digital support workflow that asks for a review after each job, handles nonresponses, and routes negative feedback privately without suppressing legitimate reviews.\n3. Draft outreach for the 8 Australian prospects, personalised to each business and its recent review activity.\n4. Define the old-list pilot agreement, attribution method, revenue-share calculation, consent checks, campaign limits, and reporting.\n5. Build a sales one-pager for the website plus Google profile rebuild aimed at fabrication and engineering shops in Laverton, Derrimut, and Dandenong.\n6. Set success measures for booked calls, review request conversion, new reviews, reactivated revenue, website enquiries, and gross margin.\n7. Produce a seven-day launch checklist with owners and dependencies.\n\nKeep the offer practical and easy to sell. Use the product name digital support. Write all customer-facing copy without em dashes."
